-- Nursing Exam Room: full rationales for CCN Mock Exam — Day 3, part B (questions 101–200)
-- Run this once in the Supabase SQL Editor. It is safe to run again.
--
-- Each explanation now says: the correct answer and why, why each other option is wrong,
-- and a key point to remember. The same questions in the practice app get the new explanation too.
-- Questions are found by their exact wording and correct answer. Where the old answer key was wrong,
-- the correct option is also fixed (listed at the top of the file).


with r(n, q, answer, exp, fixed_answer) as (values
(101, $x$Kinking of the endotracheal tube, another cause of obstruction, is most likely to occur:$x$, $x$At the point where the tube exits the mouth or nose if not properly secured, or if the patient's head is turned sharply$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: At the point where the tube exits the mouth or nose if not properly secured, or if the patient's head is turned sharply
ET tube KINKING usually happens where the tube EXITS the MOUTH or NOSE, especially if it isn't secured properly or the patient's head is turned sharply. Kinking causes high airway pressures and poor ventilation.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Only inside the ventilator circuit — Kinking isn't only inside the circuit.
• In the trachea below the carina — It doesn't occur below the carina.
• Only with tracheostomy tubes — It isn't only with tracheostomy tubes.

⭐ Key point: Secure the tube properly and support the tubing with holders.$x$, null),
(102, $x$A ventilator's "sensitivity" can be set to trigger by flow or by pressure change. Flow triggering is generally preferred because it:$x$, $x$Requires less patient work of breathing to initiate a breath than pressure triggering$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Requires less patient work of breathing to initiate a breath than pressure triggering
FLOW TRIGGERING detects a small change in flow when the patient starts to breathe. It requires LESS WORK from the patient than PRESSURE TRIGGERING (which requires the patient to lower the pressure in the circuit).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Always delivers a larger tidal volume — It doesn't always deliver larger volumes.
• Eliminates the need for PEEP — It doesn't eliminate PEEP.
• Prevents all forms of asynchrony — It doesn't prevent all asynchrony.

⭐ Key point: Set sensitivity carefully to avoid auto-triggering.$x$, null),
(103, $x$Chronic bronchitis, one of the components of COPD, is clinically defined as:$x$, $x$A productive cough occurring on most days for at least 3 months in each of 2 consecutive years$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: A productive cough occurring on most days for at least 3 months in each of 2 consecutive years
CHRONIC BRONCHITIS is defined clinically as a PRODUCTIVE COUGH occurring on most days for at least 3 MONTHS in each of 2 CONSECUTIVE YEARS, after other causes are excluded.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A single episode of productive cough lasting more than 2 weeks — A single 2-week episode is acute bronchitis.
• Cough occurring only at night for one month — Night cough for 1 month doesn't meet the definition.
• Wheeze without any cough for 6 months — Wheeze without cough doesn't meet the definition.

⭐ Key point: Smoking is the main cause; stopping smoking is the most effective intervention.$x$, null),
(104, $x$What musculoskeletal complication is common with chest compressions during CPR?$x$, $x$Rib fractures and/or sternal fracture$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Rib fractures and/or sternal fracture
Effective CHEST COMPRESSIONS in adults must be 5–6 cm deep at 100–120 per minute. This force commonly causes RIB FRACTURES and/or STERNAL FRACTURES, especially in older people with brittle bones. Other possible injuries: costochondral separation, lung contusion, and (rarely) liver or spleen injury. These injuries are ACCEPTABLE because effective CPR is life-saving — never reduce compression depth because of fear of fractures.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Skull fracture — Skull fractures are not caused by chest compressions.
• Pelvic fracture — Pelvic fractures are not caused by chest compressions.
• Femur fracture — Femur fractures are not caused by chest compressions.

⭐ Key point: Correct hand position (centre of the chest, lower half of the sternum) reduces the risk of injury to abdominal organs.$x$, null),
(105, $x$Before caring for a patient on a mechanical ventilator, the nurse should be familiar with all of the following EXCEPT:$x$, $x$The patient's dietary preferences prior to admission$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: The patient's dietary preferences prior to admission
Before caring for a ventilated patient, the nurse must know: the VENTILATOR MODE and SETTINGS, ALARM LIMITS and TROUBLESHOOTING, and signs of a BLOCKED or DISPLACED ET tube. The patient's DIETARY PREFERENCES before admission are not essential for ventilator safety.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The ventilator mode and settings in use — Mode and settings are essential.
• Alarm limits and troubleshooting — Alarm limits are essential.
• Signs of a blocked or displaced endotracheal tube — Tube problems are essential.

⭐ Key point: Always have a bag-valve-mask and suction at the bedside.$x$, null),
(106, $x$Hypercarbia during anaesthesia refers to:$x$, $x$Elevated blood carbon dioxide levels$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Elevated blood carbon dioxide levels
HYPERCARBIA (also called hypercapnia) means an ELEVATED level of CARBON DIOXIDE in the blood (PaCO2 above 45 mmHg). During anaesthesia, causes include: HYPOVENTILATION (opioids, residual muscle relaxants, inadequate ventilator settings), REBREATHING (exhausted CO2 absorber, faulty valves), increased CO2 PRODUCTION (fever, sepsis, MALIGNANT HYPERTHERMIA), and CO2 absorption during laparoscopy. Effects: acidosis, tachycardia, hypertension, arrhythmias, drowsiness, and raised ICP.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Low blood oxygen levels — LOW blood oxygen is HYPOXAEMIA.
• Elevated blood glucose — High blood glucose is HYPERGLYCAEMIA.
• Low blood pressure — Low BP is HYPOTENSION.

⭐ Key point: Capnography (end-tidal CO2) is the main monitor to detect hypercarbia during anaesthesia.$x$, null),
(107, $x$Fatigue combined with somnolence in a severe asthmatic is classified as:$x$, $x$A relative indication to strongly consider intubation$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: A relative indication to strongly consider intubation
In SEVERE ASTHMA, FATIGUE (exhaustion) with SOMNOLENCE (drowsiness) means the breathing muscles are tiring and CO2 is rising — the patient is heading towards respiratory failure. This is a RELATIVE INDICATION to STRONGLY CONSIDER INTUBATION (and an urgent ICU review). ABSOLUTE indications are cardiac or respiratory arrest, coma, or severe refractory hypoxaemia.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• An absolute indication for intubation — It is not an absolute indication on its own; absolute ones include arrest and coma.
• A sign of successful treatment — Drowsiness and fatigue are signs of DETERIORATION, not successful treatment.
• Not clinically significant — They are highly significant warning signs.

⭐ Key point: Other danger signs in asthma: silent chest, cyanosis, SpO2 below 92%, normal or rising PaCO2, bradycardia and hypotension.$x$, null),
(108, $x$The nurse calls for you to help extubate a female patient who is orally intubated. All of the following equipment is required for this procedure EXCEPT:$x$, $x$Arterial blood gas kit$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Arterial blood gas kit
EXTUBATION can fail suddenly (laryngospasm, airway oedema, aspiration, weakness), so emergency equipment must be ready: SUCTION equipment (to clear the mouth, throat and tube before removal), a BAG-VALVE-MASK with oxygen (to support breathing if needed), and a NEW ENDOTRACHEAL TUBE with laryngoscope (in case RE-INTUBATION is needed). Also: oxygen delivery device (face mask or nasal cannula), syringe to deflate the cuff, and monitoring. An ARTERIAL BLOOD GAS KIT is not required for the extubation procedure itself (an ABG may be checked later if needed).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• New endotracheal tube — A new ET tube IS needed for possible re-intubation.
• Suction equipment — Suction IS needed.
• Bag-valve mask — A bag-valve-mask IS needed.

⭐ Key point: After extubation, sit the patient up, give oxygen, encourage coughing, and watch closely for stridor and respiratory distress.$x$, null),
(109, $x$A patient is extubated after general anaesthesia and shortly develops high-pitched inspiratory stridor. The nurse should suspect:$x$, $x$Laryngospasm or airway oedema, requiring urgent assessment and possible intervention$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Laryngospasm or airway oedema, requiring urgent assessment and possible intervention
HIGH-PITCHED INSPIRATORY STRIDOR soon after extubation means the UPPER AIRWAY is NARROWED — usually from LARYNGOSPASM (spasm of the vocal cords) or AIRWAY OEDEMA (swelling of the larynx from the tube). It can quickly progress to complete obstruction. Urgent actions: call for help, sit the patient up, give high-flow oxygen, and consider nebulised adrenaline, IV steroids (dexamethasone), positive pressure (CPAP) for laryngospasm, and preparation for re-intubation.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Normal post-extubation voice change with no concern — Stridor is not a normal voice change; hoarseness may be normal, stridor is not.
• Improved airway patency — Stridor shows a NARROWED airway, not improved patency.
• A sign of adequate recovery — Stridor is a sign of a dangerous problem, not adequate recovery.

⭐ Key point: A "cuff leak test" before extubation can help predict post-extubation airway oedema.$x$, null),
(110, $x$Early signs of local anaesthetic systemic toxicity (LAST) include:$x$, $x$Perioral numbness, tinnitus, and metallic taste$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Perioral numbness, tinnitus, and metallic taste
LOCAL ANAESTHETIC SYSTEMIC TOXICITY (LAST) happens when local anaesthetic (especially bupivacaine) reaches high levels in the blood. EARLY signs are usually neurological: PERIORAL NUMBNESS (tingling around the mouth and tongue), TINNITUS (ringing in the ears), METALLIC TASTE, light-headedness, dizziness, visual disturbance, agitation and confusion. These can progress quickly to SEIZURES, then cardiovascular toxicity (arrhythmias, hypotension, cardiac arrest).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Bradycardia only — Bradycardia alone is a later cardiovascular sign, not an early sign.
• Hyperthermia — Hyperthermia is not typical of LAST.
• Improved level of consciousness — Consciousness WORSENS, not improves.

⭐ Key point: If LAST is suspected: stop the injection, call for help, give oxygen, treat seizures with benzodiazepines, and give 20% lipid emulsion.$x$, null),
(111, $x$The equipment needed for nasotracheal intubation includes the following EXCEPT:$x$, $x$Chromic 2/0 suture$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Chromic 2/0 suture
NASOTRACHEAL INTUBATION passes the tube through the nose into the trachea (used in oral/maxillofacial surgery or when the mouth can't be opened). Equipment includes: LARYNGOSCOPE (to see the vocal cords), ENDOTRACHEAL TUBE (often a smaller, softer nasal tube), MAGILL FORCEPS (to guide the tube tip through the vocal cords), lubricant, nasal vasoconstrictor spray (to reduce bleeding), suction and oxygen. A CHROMIC 2/0 SUTURE (an absorbable surgical stitch) is NOT needed.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Magill forceps — Magill forceps ARE needed.
• Laryngoscope — A laryngoscope IS needed.
• Endotracheal tube — An ET tube IS needed.

⭐ Key point: Nasotracheal intubation is contraindicated in suspected basal skull fracture and severe clotting problems.$x$, null),
(112, $x$The nurse is monitoring a sedated patient using the Bispectral Index (BIS) monitoring. The purpose of BIS monitoring is to:$x$, $x$Measure the patient's level of consciousness$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Measure the patient's level of consciousness
The BISPECTRAL INDEX (BIS) monitor uses sensors on the forehead to analyse the brain's electrical activity (EEG) and produce a number from 0 to 100 that reflects the LEVEL OF CONSCIOUSNESS (depth of sedation/anaesthesia). 100 = fully awake; 60–80 = light to moderate sedation; 40–60 = general anaesthesia (low risk of awareness); below 40 = deep hypnotic state; 0 = no brain activity. It is especially useful in PARALYSED patients, who cannot show signs of awareness.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Monitor the patient's blood pressure — BIS does not measure blood pressure.
• Assess the patient's heart rate — BIS does not measure heart rate.
• Assess the patient's muscle paralysis — Muscle paralysis is assessed with a peripheral nerve stimulator (train-of-four), not BIS.

⭐ Key point: Use BIS together with clinical assessment; muscle activity and some drugs can affect readings.$x$, null),
(113, $x$A patient recovering from anaesthesia after a lung transplant requires particular nursing vigilance for:$x$, $x$Signs of rejection and pulmonary infection$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Signs of rejection and pulmonary infection
After LUNG TRANSPLANT, patients take strong immunosuppressive drugs to prevent REJECTION, which makes them very vulnerable to INFECTION. The transplanted lung is also directly exposed to inhaled germs and has impaired cough reflex and lymphatic drainage. Nursing vigilance focuses on signs of REJECTION (breathlessness, falling SpO2, fever, new infiltrates, declining lung function) and PULMONARY INFECTION (fever, purulent sputum, new chest X-ray changes), as well as primary graft dysfunction, fluid balance and pain control.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Only cardiac monitoring, with no respiratory focus — Respiratory monitoring is essential after lung transplant, not just cardiac.
• Skin integrity exclusively — Skin care is important, but not the main focus.
• Bowel function exclusively — Bowel function is important, but not the main focus.

⭐ Key point: Strict infection control (hand hygiene, protective isolation) and early mobilisation and physiotherapy are essential.$x$, null),
(114, $x$Infraglottic inhalational injury is mainly caused by:$x$, $x$Noxious chemical combustion products in smoke$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Noxious chemical combustion products in smoke
In INHALATION INJURY, the UPPER airway (above the vocal cords — supraglottic) absorbs most of the HEAT from hot gases (it is very efficient at cooling air), so direct thermal burns are usually limited to the upper airway. INFRAGLOTTIC (below the vocal cords) injury is mainly caused by NOXIOUS CHEMICAL COMBUSTION PRODUCTS in SMOKE — e.g. aldehydes, ammonia, sulphur dioxide, chlorine, and acids from burning plastics — which irritate and damage the lining of the trachea, bronchi and alveoli, causing bronchospasm, oedema, sloughing of the mucosa, and ARDS. (Steam is an exception — it carries heat lower.)

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Direct heat — Direct heat mainly injures the UPPER (supraglottic) airway.
• Blunt trauma — Blunt trauma is not the mechanism.
• Radiation exposure — Radiation exposure is not the mechanism.

⭐ Key point: Suspect inhalation injury with burns in an enclosed space, soot in the mouth or sputum, hoarseness, and facial burns.$x$, null),
(115, $x$What percentage of solitary thyroid nodules are malignant?$x$, $x$15%$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 15%
SOLITARY THYROID NODULES are common (especially in women and older people). Most are benign (e.g. colloid nodules, adenomas, cysts). About 5–15% are MALIGNANT (thyroid cancer). The risk is higher in men, children, older adults, people with a history of radiation to the neck, rapidly growing or hard, fixed nodules, hoarseness (recurrent laryngeal nerve involvement), and enlarged neck lymph nodes.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 1% — 1% underestimates the cancer risk.
• 5% — 5% is the lower end of the range.
• 50% — 50% greatly overestimates.

⭐ Key point: Assess nodules with thyroid function tests, ultrasound, and fine-needle aspiration (FNA) cytology.$x$, null),
(116, $x$Which is NOT a marker of acute organ dysfunction?$x$, $x$Decreased creatinine level$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Decreased creatinine level
ACUTE ORGAN DYSFUNCTION (as in sepsis) is shown by markers such as: JAUNDICE (raised bilirubin — liver dysfunction), THROMBOCYTOPENIA (low platelets — haematological/clotting dysfunction), HYPOTENSION (cardiovascular dysfunction), RAISED CREATININE or low urine output (kidney dysfunction), low GCS/confusion (brain dysfunction), and low PaO2/FiO2 (lung dysfunction). A DECREASED CREATININE does NOT indicate organ dysfunction — kidney dysfunction causes creatinine to RISE.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Jaundice — Jaundice IS a marker of liver dysfunction.
• Thrombocytopenia — Thrombocytopenia IS a marker of haematological dysfunction.
• Hypotension — Hypotension IS a marker of cardiovascular dysfunction.

⭐ Key point: The SOFA score uses these markers to grade organ dysfunction in sepsis.$x$, null),
(117, $x$Systemic burn shock risk begins above what %TBSA?$x$, $x$>20%$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: >20%
Burns cause local tissue damage, but large burns trigger a SYSTEMIC inflammatory response: capillaries throughout the body become LEAKY, and large amounts of fluid and protein move out of the blood vessels into the tissues ("burn shock" — a form of hypovolaemic and distributive shock). This systemic response usually begins when burns exceed about 20% TBSA in adults (some guidelines use 15%; about 10% in children and elderly). These patients need formal IV fluid resuscitation (e.g. Parkland formula).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• >5% — 5% TBSA is too small to cause systemic shock in most adults.
• >10% — 10% is the usual threshold in children, not adults.
• >50% — 50% is far above the threshold.

⭐ Key point: Monitor urine output hourly (target 0.5–1 mL/kg/hr in adults) to guide fluid resuscitation.$x$, null),
(118, $x$A patient with Clostridioides difficile infection requires which type of isolation precaution?$x$, $x$Contact precautions, with gown and gloves, and hand hygiene using soap and water (not alcohol-based sanitiser alone) given spore resistance$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Contact precautions, with gown and gloves, and hand hygiene using soap and water (not alcohol-based sanitiser alone) given spore resistance
CLOSTRIDIOIDES DIFFICILE (C. diff) causes diarrhoea and colitis, often after antibiotic use. It spreads by CONTACT (faecal–oral route via hands, equipment and surfaces) and forms SPORES that survive for months in the environment and are NOT killed by alcohol hand rub. Precautions: CONTACT PRECAUTIONS — single room with its own toilet, GLOVES and GOWN/apron for all contact, dedicated equipment — plus HAND HYGIENE with SOAP AND WATER (which physically removes spores), and cleaning with chlorine-based (sporicidal) disinfectant.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Airborne precautions with N95 respirator — C. diff is not airborne; N95 respirators are not needed.
• No precautions are necessary — Precautions ARE necessary — it spreads easily.
• Droplet precautions only — It is not spread by droplets.

⭐ Key point: Stop unnecessary antibiotics (especially broad-spectrum ones) and avoid anti-diarrhoeal drugs in C. diff infection.$x$, null),
(119, $x$A patient with obstructive shock from massive pulmonary embolism would be expected to show:$x$, $x$Low blood pressure with signs of right heart strain (e.g. elevated JVP) from obstructed pulmonary blood flow$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Low blood pressure with signs of right heart strain (e.g. elevated JVP) from obstructed pulmonary blood flow
In OBSTRUCTIVE SHOCK from MASSIVE PE, blood can't flow through the lungs. The right ventricle is strained and fails, causing LOW BP, RAISED JVP, tachycardia and hypoxia.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Warm, flushed skin with bounding pulses — Warm, flushed skin is typical of septic shock.
• Bradycardia with normal blood pressure — Bradycardia with normal BP is not typical.
• No cardiovascular compromise — Massive PE causes compromise.

⭐ Key point: Treat with thrombolysis if unstable.$x$, null),
(120, $x$Which of the following is not a cause of polytrauma?$x$, $x$Infection$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Infection
POLYTRAUMA means multiple significant injuries to different body regions (e.g. head, chest, abdomen, limbs), usually from high-energy mechanisms: BOMB BLASTS (blast, shrapnel, burns, crush), FALLS FROM HEIGHT, road traffic ACCIDENTS (cars, motorcycles — very common in Nigeria), assaults, gunshots, and building collapses. INFECTION is a disease process caused by microorganisms, not a physical force, so it is NOT a cause of trauma (though infection can be a complication of trauma).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Bomb blast — Bomb blasts ARE a cause of polytrauma.
• Fall from height — Falls from height ARE a cause.
• Accident — Accidents ARE a cause.

⭐ Key point: Manage polytrauma with the ATLS approach: primary survey (ABCDE), resuscitation, then secondary survey.$x$, null),
(121, $x$The main complication of FHF, occurring in about 80% of cases, is:$x$, $x$Cerebral oedema$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Cerebral oedema
FULMINANT HEPATIC FAILURE (FHF) is rapid liver failure with encephalopathy in a person without previous liver disease (e.g. from paracetamol overdose or viral hepatitis). The main and most dangerous complication is CEREBRAL OEDEMA (brain swelling), which develops in up to about 80% of patients with severe (grade 3–4) encephalopathy. Ammonia and other toxins that the failing liver cannot clear cause brain cells (astrocytes) to swell. This raises intracranial pressure and can cause brain herniation and death.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Renal calculi — Renal calculi (kidney stones) are not a typical complication.
• Pancreatitis — Pancreatitis is not a typical complication.
• Peptic ulcer — Peptic ulcer is not the main complication.

⭐ Key point: Manage with head-up 30°, avoid hypoxia and fever, control ammonia, consider hypertonic saline, and refer early for liver transplant.$x$, null),
(122, $x$During a multi-vehicle road traffic accident with 15 casualties and limited ambulances, the triage officer applies START triage. A casualty who is breathing at 35/min after airway repositioning is categorised as:$x$, $x$Immediate (red) — respiratory rate outside the normal range signals urgent priority$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Immediate (red) — respiratory rate outside the normal range signals urgent priority
START TRIAGE (Simple Triage and Rapid Treatment) is used in mass casualties. Steps: (1) Can the patient WALK? If yes → GREEN (minor). (2) Is the patient BREATHING? If no after airway repositioning → BLACK. (3) Respiratory rate above 30/min → RED (IMMEDIATE). (4) Check perfusion (capillary refill over 2 s or no radial pulse) → RED. (5) Can't follow simple commands → RED. Otherwise → YELLOW (delayed). A casualty breathing at 35/min is categorised as IMMEDIATE (RED).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Minor (green) — can wait — Green is for the walking wounded.
• Deceased (black) — Black is for those not breathing after airway repositioning.
• Delayed (yellow) — Yellow is for those who can wait but are not walking.

⭐ Key point: START aims to do the greatest good for the greatest number with limited resources.$x$, null),
(123, $x$Calcitonin is a tumour marker specifically associated with which type of thyroid cancer?$x$, $x$Medullary$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Medullary
CALCITONIN is a hormone made by the PARAFOLLICULAR (C) CELLS of the thyroid, which help regulate calcium. MEDULLARY THYROID CANCER arises from these C cells and produces large amounts of calcitonin, so CALCITONIN is used as a TUMOUR MARKER for diagnosis and follow-up (along with CEA). Medullary thyroid cancer can be inherited as part of MULTIPLE ENDOCRINE NEOPLASIA type 2 (MEN 2), linked to the RET gene.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Papillary — Papillary cancer arises from follicular cells; thyroglobulin is used for follow-up.
• Follicular — Follicular cancer arises from follicular cells; thyroglobulin is used.
• Anaplastic — Anaplastic cancer has no specific tumour marker.

⭐ Key point: Patients with medullary thyroid cancer should be screened for phaeochromocytoma before surgery (MEN 2).$x$, null),
(124, $x$A patient with severe burns and multisystem trauma in a resource-limited disaster is triaged as black because:$x$, $x$A and C$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: A and C
In DISASTER TRIAGE (mass casualties with limited resources), a BLACK (EXPECTANT) category is used for patients whose injuries are so severe that SURVIVAL is UNLIKELY even with treatment, AND treating them would use up SCARCE RESOURCES (staff, blood, theatre time) needed to save more people with survivable injuries. So "A AND C" is correct. These patients still receive comfort care (pain relief) and are reassessed if resources become available.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Survival is unlikely — Unlikely survival alone is only part of the reason.
• The patient is deceased — Being deceased is also categorised as black, but this patient is described as alive.
• Resources are needed elsewhere — Resource needs alone is only part of the reason.

⭐ Key point: Disaster triage shifts from "best for each patient" to "greatest good for the greatest number".$x$, null),
(125, $x$The first clinical symptom of tetanus is typically:$x$, $x$Trismus (lockjaw)$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Trismus (lockjaw)
TETANUS is caused by a toxin from Clostridium tetani (from contaminated wounds). The toxin blocks inhibitory nerve signals, causing muscle spasm and rigidity. The FIRST symptom is usually TRISMUS (LOCKJAW) — spasm of the jaw muscles (masseters), making it hard to open the mouth or chew. This is followed by stiffness of the neck and back, risus sardonicus (a fixed, grimacing smile), difficulty swallowing, abdominal rigidity, painful generalised spasms (opisthotonus) and autonomic instability.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Generalised convulsions — Generalised convulsions (spasms) occur LATER.
• Risus sardonicus — Risus sardonicus occurs after trismus.
• Neck stiffness — Neck stiffness may occur early but trismus is classically first.

⭐ Key point: Treatment: human tetanus immunoglobulin, wound debridement, metronidazole, muscle relaxants/sedation (benzodiazepines, magnesium), airway support, and a quiet, dark environment. Prevent by vaccination.$x$, null),
(126, $x$CVP that is low most likely indicates the need for:$x$, $x$Fluid administration$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Fluid administration
CENTRAL VENOUS PRESSURE (CVP) reflects the pressure in the right atrium and is used as a rough guide to the patient's blood volume and right heart filling. Normal CVP is about 2–8 mmHg (or 3–10 cmH2O). A LOW CVP suggests HYPOVOLAEMIA (low blood volume — e.g. dehydration, bleeding, vasodilation), so FLUID ADMINISTRATION (fluid challenge) is usually indicated, with careful reassessment of BP, heart rate, urine output and CVP.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Diuretics — Diuretics would remove more fluid and worsen hypovolaemia.
• Vasoconstrictors only — Vasoconstrictors alone don't correct a low volume; fluids are needed first.
• No action needed — A low CVP with signs of poor perfusion needs action.

⭐ Key point: CVP trends and the response to a fluid challenge are more useful than a single reading.$x$, null),
(127, $x$A Mallory-Weiss tear classically occurs after:$x$, $x$Repeated vomiting$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Repeated vomiting
A MALLORY-WEISS TEAR is a tear in the lining (mucosa) at the junction of the oesophagus and stomach, caused by a sudden rise in pressure during REPEATED VOMITING or forceful retching — often after heavy alcohol intake, or in hyperemesis gravidarum or bulimia. It typically causes vomiting of bright red blood (haematemesis) after episodes of non-bloody vomiting. Most tears heal on their own within a few days; endoscopic treatment is needed if bleeding continues.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Chronic constipation — Chronic constipation is not a cause.
• A single episode of coughing — A single episode of coughing is unlikely to cause it.
• Eating spicy food — Eating spicy food does not cause it.

⭐ Key point: Distinguish from Boerhaave syndrome (full-thickness oesophageal rupture), which causes severe chest pain and surgical emphysema.$x$, null),
(128, $x$In mass casualty triage systems such as START, a casualty who is not breathing even after repositioning the airway is typically categorised as:$x$, $x$Deceased/expectant (black)$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Deceased/expectant (black)
In START TRIAGE, a casualty who is NOT BREATHING even AFTER REPOSITIONING THE AIRWAY (head tilt/jaw thrust) is categorised as DECEASED/EXPECTANT (BLACK). In a mass casualty situation, resources are directed to people with survivable injuries; CPR is not usually started in this setting because it uses up many responders for one patient with a very low chance of survival. If the patient starts breathing after airway repositioning, they are categorised as IMMEDIATE (RED).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Immediate (red) — Immediate (red) is for those who breathe after airway repositioning or have other serious signs.
• Delayed (yellow) — Delayed (yellow) is for those who can wait.
• Minor (green) — Minor (green) is for the walking wounded.

⭐ Key point: Triage categories are reassessed as the situation and resources change.$x$, null),
(129, $x$The pyramidal lobe of the thyroid, when present, is a remnant of the:$x$, $x$Thyroglossal duct$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Thyroglossal duct
During development, the THYROID gland forms at the base of the tongue and migrates down the neck to its final position in front of the trachea, leaving behind a tube called the THYROGLOSSAL DUCT, which usually disappears. In about 40–50% of people, a small part of thyroid tissue remains along this path, forming the PYRAMIDAL LOBE, which extends upwards from the isthmus. Remnants can also form THYROGLOSSAL CYSTS (midline neck lumps that move upward when sticking out the tongue).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Parathyroid gland — It is not related to the parathyroid glands.
• Carotid sheath — It is not related to the carotid sheath.
• Trachea — It is not related to the trachea.

⭐ Key point: The pyramidal lobe may enlarge in Graves' disease and can be seen on thyroid scans.$x$, null),
(130, $x$Sepsis is currently defined (Sepsis-3) as:$x$, $x$Life-threatening organ dysfunction caused by a dysregulated host response to infection$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Life-threatening organ dysfunction caused by a dysregulated host response to infection
SEPSIS-3 (2016) defines SEPSIS as LIFE-THREATENING ORGAN DYSFUNCTION caused by a DYSREGULATED HOST RESPONSE to INFECTION. Organ dysfunction is identified by an acute rise in the SOFA score of 2 or more points. SEPTIC SHOCK is a subset with circulatory and metabolic abnormalities: needing vasopressors to keep MAP ≥65 mmHg AND lactate above 2 mmol/L despite adequate fluids. The older SIRS-based definitions are no longer used.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Any infection, regardless of organ function — Infection without organ dysfunction is not sepsis.
• A fever above 38°C alone — Fever alone does not define sepsis.
• Bacteraemia without any clinical symptoms — Bacteraemia without symptoms is not sepsis.

⭐ Key point: Use qSOFA (respiratory rate ≥22, altered mentation, systolic BP ≤100) as a quick bedside screen outside ICU.$x$, null),
(131, $x$The female-to-male ratio in goitre incidence is approximately:$x$, $x$10:1$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 10:1
GOITRE (enlargement of the thyroid gland) is much more common in WOMEN than men. The female-to-male ratio is often quoted as about 4:1 up to 10:1, depending on the type and population. Reasons include the effects of oestrogen and pregnancy (increased iodine needs) and the higher rate of autoimmune thyroid disease (Graves', Hashimoto's) in women. Iodine deficiency is the most common cause of goitre worldwide.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 1:1 — 1:1 is incorrect — goitre is not equally common.
• 2:1 — 2:1 underestimates the difference.
• 5:1 — 5:1 is within the range but lower than the figure used in this exam.

⭐ Key point: Iodised salt has greatly reduced goitre in many countries, including Nigeria.$x$, null),
(132, $x$A nurse is providing psychological support to a burn patient. The nurse should be aware that the patient may experience which of the following emotional responses?$x$, $x$Anxiety, depression, and body image disturbance$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Anxiety, depression, and body image disturbance
Major burns are physically and emotionally traumatic. Patients commonly experience ANXIETY (fear of pain, procedures, death), DEPRESSION, POST-TRAUMATIC STRESS (flashbacks, nightmares), sleep problems, grief and loss, and BODY IMAGE DISTURBANCE (scarring, disfigurement, loss of function, changes in appearance). These responses can affect recovery, adherence to treatment and return to normal life.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A sense of euphoria — Euphoria is not a typical response.
• Apathy and indifference — Apathy may occur, but anxiety, depression and body image problems are more typical.
• A rapid return to normal daily life — Recovery is usually long and difficult, not a rapid return to normal.

⭐ Key point: Provide early psychological support, adequate pain relief, honest information, family involvement, and referral to counselling or support groups.$x$, null),
(133, $x$A patient with an electrical burn injury passes dark, tea-coloured urine. The nurse should suspect:$x$, $x$Myoglobinuria from muscle breakdown, requiring aggressive fluid therapy and monitoring for acute kidney injury$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Myoglobinuria from muscle breakdown, requiring aggressive fluid therapy and monitoring for acute kidney injury
High-voltage ELECTRICAL injuries cause extensive DEEP MUSCLE DAMAGE along the path of the current (even when skin burns look small). Damaged muscle releases MYOGLOBIN into the blood (RHABDOMYOLYSIS), which turns urine DARK, TEA- or COLA-COLOURED (MYOGLOBINURIA). Myoglobin can block the kidney tubules and cause ACUTE KIDNEY INJURY. Treatment: AGGRESSIVE IV FLUIDS (aim for urine output 1–2 mL/kg/hr until the urine clears), monitoring of creatinine, CK and potassium, and possibly mannitol and bicarbonate per protocol.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A normal finding requiring no action — Dark urine after electrical injury is NOT normal.
• A urinary tract infection only — A urinary tract infection would not explain this picture.
• Dehydration alone with no other significance — Dehydration may concentrate urine, but tea-coloured urine after electrical injury suggests myoglobinuria.

⭐ Key point: Also watch for hyperkalaemia, cardiac arrhythmias (continuous ECG), and compartment syndrome.$x$, null),
(134, $x$The correct order of the ABCDE primary survey in trauma/emergency assessment is:$x$, $x$Airway, Breathing, Circulation, Disability, Exposure$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Airway, Breathing, Circulation, Disability, Exposure
The ABCDE PRIMARY SURVEY assesses and treats life-threatening problems in the order they kill: A = AIRWAY (with cervical spine protection in trauma); B = BREATHING (ventilation and oxygenation); C = CIRCULATION (with haemorrhage control); D = DISABILITY (neurological status: GCS/AVPU, pupils, glucose); E = EXPOSURE (fully undress to look for injuries, while preventing hypothermia). Problems are treated as they are found before moving on.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Breathing, Airway, Disability, Circulation, Exposure — Breathing cannot be effective without an open airway, so airway comes first.
• Circulation, Airway, Breathing, Exposure, Disability — Circulation is not assessed before airway and breathing (except catastrophic haemorrhage in some military/pre-hospital systems — "C-ABCDE").
• Disability, Airway, Breathing, Circulation, Exposure — Disability is assessed after ABC.

⭐ Key point: Reassess ABCDE after every intervention and whenever the patient's condition changes.$x$, null),
(135, $x$Primary postpartum haemorrhage following a caesarean section is defined as blood loss of:$x$, $x$≥1000ml$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: ≥1000ml
POSTPARTUM HAEMORRHAGE (PPH) is defined by the amount of blood loss within 24 hours of birth: after VAGINAL birth — 500 mL or more; after CAESAREAN SECTION — 1,000 mL or more (because more blood is normally lost during surgery). Major PPH is often defined as more than 1,000 mL, and severe as more than 2,000 mL. Any blood loss causing signs of shock is PPH regardless of the amount.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• ≥250ml — 250 mL is a normal blood loss.
• ≥500ml — 500 mL is the threshold for vaginal birth.
• ≥3000ml — 3,000 mL is massive haemorrhage, far above the definition threshold.

⭐ Key point: Blood loss is often underestimated — weigh pads and drapes, and watch vital signs.$x$, null),
(136, $x$Clinical features of hyperemesis gravidarum include all EXCEPT:$x$, $x$Insomnia$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Insomnia
HYPEREMESIS GRAVIDARUM causes severe, persistent nausea and vomiting in pregnancy. Typical features: VOMITING many times a day (more than 3–4 episodes), inability to keep food or fluids down, WEIGHT LOSS of more than 5% of pre-pregnancy weight, DEHYDRATION (DIZZINESS, dry mouth, low urine output, tachycardia, postural hypotension), ketonuria, electrolyte imbalance (low potassium, low sodium), and fatigue. INSOMNIA (inability to sleep) is not a typical clinical feature.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Vomiting more than 3–4 times a day — Frequent vomiting IS a feature.
• Weight loss of more than 5% — Weight loss of more than 5% IS a feature.
• Dizziness — Dizziness (from dehydration) IS a feature.

⭐ Key point: Treat with IV fluids, anti-emetics, electrolyte replacement, thiamine, and thromboprophylaxis if admitted.$x$, null),
(137, $x$A newborn requires resuscitation immediately after birth and is not breathing adequately. The priority initial action is to:$x$, $x$Dry, stimulate, and position the airway, then provide positive pressure ventilation if breathing remains inadequate$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Dry, stimulate, and position the airway, then provide positive pressure ventilation if breathing remains inadequate
Most newborns who need help at birth have a BREATHING problem. The first steps (within the first 30–60 seconds) are: DRY the baby (and keep warm), STIMULATE (drying and rubbing), and POSITION THE AIRWAY (head in neutral position, clear the airway if obstructed). If the baby is still NOT BREATHING ADEQUATELY (apnoea, gasping) or the heart rate is below 100/min, give POSITIVE PRESSURE VENTILATION (inflation breaths with a bag-valve-mask or T-piece). Effective ventilation usually improves the heart rate quickly.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Wait several minutes before intervening — Waiting several minutes allows hypoxic brain injury.
• Immediately begin chest compressions before assessing breathing — Chest compressions come after ventilation, only if the heart rate stays below 60/min.
• Give oxygen by face mask only, with no other assessment — Face mask oxygen alone does not help a baby who is not breathing.

⭐ Key point: The "golden minute": begin ventilation within the first minute after birth if needed.$x$, null),
(138, $x$Warning signs that may precede an eclamptic convulsion include:$x$, $x$Severe headache and blurred vision$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Severe headache and blurred vision
WARNING SIGNS of impending ECLAMPSIA (seizures) in pre-eclampsia include: SEVERE HEADACHE (often frontal, not relieved by simple painkillers), VISUAL DISTURBANCES (BLURRED VISION, flashing lights, spots), epigastric or right upper quadrant pain (liver involvement), nausea and vomiting, HYPERREFLEXIA and CLONUS, sudden oedema of the face and hands, and rising BP. These signs mean the brain is irritated and seizures may soon occur.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Improved appetite — Improved appetite is not a warning sign.
• Reduced blood pressure — BP usually RISES, not falls.
• Increased urine output only — Urine output usually FALLS (oliguria), not increases.

⭐ Key point: Give magnesium sulphate, control BP, and plan for delivery when these warning signs appear.$x$, null),
(139, $x$The following are complications of eclampsia except$x$, $x$Sepsis$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Sepsis
ECLAMPSIA (seizures in pre-eclampsia) can cause serious complications: NEUROLOGICAL DEFICIT (stroke, cerebral haemorrhage, cerebral oedema, blindness), PULMONARY OEDEMA, ACUTE RENAL FAILURE, HELLP syndrome, DIC, liver rupture, placental abruption, aspiration pneumonia, and maternal and fetal death. SEPSIS is an infection and is NOT a direct complication of eclampsia (although it can occur in any hospitalised patient).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Neurological deficit — Neurological deficit IS a complication.
• Pulmonary oedema — Pulmonary oedema IS a complication.
• Acute renal failure — Acute renal failure IS a complication.

⭐ Key point: Eclampsia is a leading cause of maternal death in Nigeria; early antenatal care and BP monitoring help prevent it.$x$, null),
(140, $x$In the Pritchard regimen of MgSO4, which is correct?$x$, $x$The maintenance dose is 5 g IM 4-hourly$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: The maintenance dose is 5 g IM 4-hourly
The PRITCHARD REGIMEN of MgSO4: loading dose 4 g IV + 10 g IM (5 g in each buttock), then MAINTENANCE of 5 g IM every 4 HOURS in alternate buttocks.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 2 g/hour IV works faster — 2 g/hour IV is not the Pritchard regimen.
• 5 g is the total loading dose — The loading dose is 14 g total, not 5 g.
• It is the only accepted regimen — Other regimens (e.g. Zuspan) are also accepted.

⭐ Key point: Check reflexes, breathing and urine output before each dose.$x$, null),
(141, $x$Umbilical cord prolapse is an obstetric emergency because it can cause:$x$, $x$Cord compression leading to fetal hypoxia$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Cord compression leading to fetal hypoxia
UMBILICAL CORD PROLAPSE happens when the cord slips down through the cervix BEFORE (or beside) the baby's presenting part, usually after the membranes rupture. The baby's head or body then presses the cord against the pelvis, COMPRESSING the umbilical vessels, and the cold air and handling cause the cord vessels to spasm. Blood flow to the baby is cut off, causing FETAL HYPOXIA, bradycardia and death or brain damage within minutes unless delivery is rapid. Risk factors: malpresentation (breech, transverse), polyhydramnios, preterm baby, multiple pregnancy, and artificial rupture of membranes with a high head.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Improved fetal oxygenation — Cord prolapse REDUCES, not improves, fetal oxygenation.
• Maternal hypertension only — It does not cause maternal hypertension.
• No risk if the mother is asymptomatic — The danger is to the baby even if the mother feels well.

⭐ Key point: Emergency actions: call for help, keep the presenting part off the cord (manually or by filling the bladder), knee–chest or exaggerated Sims' position, avoid handling the cord, and arrange immediate delivery (usually caesarean section).$x$, null),
(142, $x$Which of the following is part of the definition of hyperemesis gravidarum?$x$, $x$Severe nausea and vomiting with weight loss greater than 5% of pregpregnacy body weight$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Severe nausea and vomiting with weight loss greater than 5% of pregpregnacy body weight
HYPEREMESIS GRAVIDARUM is defined as SEVERE, persistent NAUSEA and VOMITING in pregnancy causing WEIGHT LOSS of MORE THAN 5% of pre-pregnancy body weight, together with dehydration, ketosis and/or electrolyte imbalance. It usually starts before 20 weeks (often 4–10 weeks) and often improves by 16–20 weeks, although in some women it continues longer.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Vomiting that persists for the entire pregnancy — It does not have to persist for the entire pregnancy (although it can in a few women).
• Vomiting upon arising more than five days per week — Vomiting on waking several days a week describes ordinary morning sickness, not the defining criteria.
• Vomiting that appears after the 20th week of pregnancy — Vomiting that starts after 20 weeks suggests another cause (e.g. pre-eclampsia, HELLP, acute fatty liver, infection), not hyperemesis.

⭐ Key point: Assess hydration (urine ketones, electrolytes), give IV fluids, anti-emetics and thiamine, and consider thromboprophylaxis.$x$, null),
(143, $x$A gravid uterus at term weighs about:$x$, $x$1000 g$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 1000 g
The non-pregnant uterus weighs only about 50–70 g. During pregnancy, its muscle cells grow enormously (hypertrophy) and stretch, so that by TERM the uterus (without its contents) weighs about 1,000 g (1 kg) — roughly a 15–20-fold increase. Its capacity also increases from about 10 mL to about 5 litres. After delivery, it shrinks back (involution) over about 6 weeks.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 60 g — 60 g is the weight of the NON-pregnant uterus.
• 100 g — 100 g is far too small for a term uterus.
• 500 g — 500 g is too small for a term uterus (it's about the weight 1 week after delivery).

⭐ Key point: Assess uterine involution after delivery: the fundus should be firm and descend about 1 cm (one finger-breadth) per day.$x$, null),
(144, $x$Amniotic fluid embolism classically presents with the sudden onset of:$x$, $x$Acute hypoxia, hypotension, and coagulopathy during or shortly after labour$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Acute hypoxia, hypotension, and coagulopathy during or shortly after labour
AMNIOTIC FLUID EMBOLISM (AFE) is a rare but catastrophic obstetric emergency occurring DURING LABOUR, delivery or SHORTLY AFTER. Amniotic fluid enters the maternal circulation, triggering a massive inflammatory reaction. It classically presents SUDDENLY with: ACUTE HYPOXIA (breathlessness, cyanosis, respiratory arrest), HYPOTENSION/cardiovascular collapse (often cardiac arrest), and COAGULOPATHY (DIC — severe bleeding). Seizures or confusion may also occur.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Gradual, mild shortness of breath over days — AFE is SUDDEN, not gradual over days.
• Isolated mild fever — Isolated mild fever is not a feature.
• Chronic hypertension — Chronic hypertension is unrelated.

⭐ Key point: Management is supportive and urgent: call for help, CPR, oxygen/ventilation, vasopressors, massive transfusion with clotting factors, and delivery of the baby.$x$, null),
(145, $x$Oesophageal atresia in a newborn classically presents with:$x$, $x$Excessive drooling, choking and coughing with feeding attempts$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Excessive drooling, choking and coughing with feeding attempts
OESOPHAGEAL ATRESIA means the oesophagus ends in a blind pouch, so swallowed saliva and milk cannot reach the stomach. Classic signs in a newborn: EXCESSIVE DROOLING/frothy saliva from the mouth, CHOKING, COUGHING and CYANOSIS with FEEDING attempts, and respiratory distress (from aspiration — especially if there is a tracheo-oesophageal fistula). A nasogastric tube cannot be passed into the stomach (it coils in the pouch on X-ray). There may be a history of polyhydramnios in pregnancy.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Normal feeding with no complications — Normal feeding is impossible.
• Delayed passage of meconium only — Delayed passage of meconium suggests Hirschsprung's disease or meconium ileus.
• No respiratory symptoms at all — Respiratory symptoms are common because of aspiration.

⭐ Key point: Stop oral feeds, nurse head-up, use continuous suction of the upper pouch (Replogle tube), and arrange urgent surgical repair.$x$, null),
(146, $x$Which action best prevents heat loss by evaporation in a newborn?$x$, $x$Drying the infant with a warm blanket$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Drying the infant with a warm blanket
Newborns lose heat in four ways: EVAPORATION (amniotic fluid evaporating from wet skin — the biggest loss immediately after birth), conduction (contact with cold surfaces), convection (cold air currents), and radiation (heat radiating to cold walls/objects). The best way to prevent EVAPORATIVE heat loss is to DRY THE INFANT thoroughly and immediately with a WARM BLANKET/towel (and then replace the wet towel with a dry one), and place the baby skin-to-skin with the mother, with a hat.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Warming the crib pad — Warming the crib pad prevents CONDUCTIVE heat loss.
• Closing the doors to the room — Closing doors prevents CONVECTIVE heat loss (draughts).
• Turning on the overhead radiant warmer — A radiant warmer reduces RADIANT heat loss.

⭐ Key point: Hypothermia in newborns causes hypoglycaemia, acidosis and breathing problems — keep the "warm chain".$x$, null),
(147, $x$A nurse is caring for a newborn in the NICU and suspects neonatal sepsis. Which of the following is an early sign of neonatal sepsis?$x$, $x$Poor feeding, lethargy, and respiratory distress$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Poor feeding, lethargy, and respiratory distress
NEONATAL SEPSIS often presents with SUBTLE, NON-SPECIFIC signs because newborns have an immature immune system. Early signs: POOR FEEDING, LETHARGY (reduced activity, floppiness), RESPIRATORY DISTRESS (tachypnoea, grunting, apnoea), temperature instability (often HYPOthermia rather than fever), jaundice, vomiting, poor colour or mottling, and abnormal glucose levels.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A high-grade fever — High-grade fever is not typical — newborns often have low or unstable temperatures.
• A stable blood sugar level — Stable blood sugar is reassuring, not a sign of sepsis (sepsis often causes hypo- or hyperglycaemia).
• A sudden increase in activity — Increased activity is not typical; lethargy is more common.

⭐ Key point: Any newborn who is "not right" should be assessed for sepsis; take cultures and start IV antibiotics early.$x$, null),
(148, $x$First action in a precipitous delivery?$x$, $x$Establish an airway for the newborn$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Establish an airway for the newborn
In a PRECIPITOUS (very rapid, unplanned) DELIVERY, the FIRST action after the baby is born is to ESTABLISH AN AIRWAY for the newborn — clear the mouth and nose if needed (wipe or gentle suction), dry and stimulate the baby, and ensure breathing. Breathing is the most critical need for survival.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Arrange transport — Arranging transport can wait until the mother and baby are stable.
• Ascertain condition of fundus — Checking the fundus is important for the mother after the placenta is delivered, but the newborn's airway comes first.
• Tie and cut cord — Tying and cutting the cord is not urgent (delayed cord clamping is beneficial if the baby is well).

⭐ Key point: Keep the baby warm (skin-to-skin), and watch the mother for postpartum haemorrhage.$x$, null),
(149, $x$Hirschsprung's disease is caused by:$x$, $x$Absence of ganglion cells in a segment of bowel, causing functional obstruction$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Absence of ganglion cells in a segment of bowel, causing functional obstruction
HIRSCHSPRUNG'S DISEASE is caused by the ABSENCE of GANGLION CELLS (nerve cells) in a segment of the bowel wall (usually the rectum and sigmoid colon). Without these nerves, the segment cannot relax and push stool along (no peristalsis), causing a FUNCTIONAL OBSTRUCTION. The bowel above becomes dilated.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Excess ganglion cells in the bowel wall — It is caused by ABSENCE, not excess, of ganglion cells.
• A viral infection of the colon — It is not caused by a viral infection.
• An allergic reaction to milk protein — It is not caused by milk protein allergy.

⭐ Key point: Diagnosed by rectal biopsy; treated surgically by removing the affected segment.$x$, null),
(150, $x$Neonatal jaundice is usually clinically visible when serum bilirubin exceeds:$x$, $x$5 mg/dL$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 5 mg/dL
NEONATAL JAUNDICE becomes VISIBLE in the skin when serum bilirubin exceeds about 5 mg/dL (about 85 µmol/L). In adults, jaundice is visible at about 2–3 mg/dL. Jaundice spreads from head to toe as levels rise.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 15 mg/dL — 15 mg/dL is a high level, not the threshold of visibility.
• 25 mg/dL — 25 mg/dL is very high and risks kernicterus.
• 50 mg/dL — 50 mg/dL is extremely high.

⭐ Key point: Visual assessment is unreliable — measure bilirubin (transcutaneous or serum) when jaundice is noted.$x$, null),
(151, $x$On the Travis illness-wellness continuum, the neutral point at the center of the scale represents what?$x$, $x$An absence of discernible signs or symptoms of disease, without the presence of positive wellness behaviors$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: An absence of discernible signs or symptoms of disease, without the presence of positive wellness behaviors
On TRAVIS'S ILLNESS-WELLNESS CONTINUUM, the NEUTRAL POINT in the middle represents an ABSENCE of discernible signs or symptoms of DISEASE, WITHOUT POSITIVE WELLNESS BEHAVIOURS. Moving left leads to illness, disability and premature death; moving right (through awareness, education and growth) leads to high-level wellness.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Death — Death is at the extreme left end.
• Peak, high-level wellness — Peak wellness is at the extreme right end.
• The point of maximum disability — Maximum disability is towards the left end.

⭐ Key point: Health promotion aims to move people towards high-level wellness, not just the absence of disease.$x$, null),
(152, $x$A nurse writes the goal: "Client will ambulate 30 meters with a walker by discharge on 07/28/2026." Which SMART element is reflected by the phrase "by discharge on 07/28/2026"?$x$, $x$Time-bound$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Time-bound
SMART goals make outcomes clear and measurable: S = SPECIFIC (clearly states what will be done — "ambulate 30 metres with a walker"); M = MEASURABLE (can be counted — "30 metres"); A = ACHIEVABLE (realistic for this client); R = RELEVANT/REALISTIC (related to the client's problem); T = TIME-BOUND (has a clear DEADLINE). The phrase "BY DISCHARGE ON 07/28/2026" sets the deadline, so it reflects the TIME-BOUND element.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Specific — "Specific" is reflected by the clear description of the activity.
• Measurable — "Measurable" is reflected by "30 metres".
• Achievable — "Achievable" is about whether the goal is realistic for this patient.

⭐ Key point: Without a time frame, it is impossible to evaluate whether a goal has been met on time.$x$, null),
(153, $x$Phaneuf's nursing audit tool evaluates nursing care under seven functions, which include all of the following EXCEPT:$x$, $x$Approval of hospital annual budget$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Approval of hospital annual budget
PHANEUF'S NURSING AUDIT evaluates nursing care through records against SEVEN FUNCTIONS of nursing: (1) application and execution of physician's legal orders; (2) observation of symptoms and reactions; (3) supervision of the patient; (4) supervision of those participating in care; (5) reporting and recording; (6) application and execution of nursing procedures and techniques; (7) promotion of physical and emotional health by direction and teaching. "APPROVAL OF HOSPITAL ANNUAL BUDGET" is NOT one of the functions.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Application and execution of physician's legal orders — Execution of physician's orders IS a function.
• Observation of symptoms and reactions — Observation of symptoms IS a function.
• Supervision of the patient — Supervision of the patient IS a function.

⭐ Key point: Nursing audits help evaluate and improve the quality of nursing care.$x$, null),
(154, $x$A charge nurse asked why a particular protocol works considers several possible explanations and weighs evidence for and against each rather than accepting the first explanation offered. This reflects:$x$, $x$Open-mindedness combined with analysis$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Open-mindedness combined with analysis
Considering SEVERAL POSSIBLE EXPLANATIONS and WEIGHING EVIDENCE for and against each, rather than accepting the first answer, shows OPEN-MINDEDNESS (willingness to consider different views) combined with ANALYSIS (systematically evaluating information). These are key critical thinking attitudes.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Overconfidence — Overconfidence would mean accepting the first answer without checking.
• Avoidance of the question — Avoidance would mean not answering at all.
• Conformity to peer opinion — Conformity would mean accepting what others think without evaluating.

⭐ Key point: Critical thinking improves clinical decision-making and patient safety.$x$, null),
(155, $x$Evaluation in the nursing process is best described as:$x$, $x$An ongoing procedure of re-assessment$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: An ongoing procedure of re-assessment
EVALUATION in the nursing process is an ONGOING process of RE-ASSESSMENT, checking whether the patient is achieving the expected outcomes, and modifying the plan as needed. It occurs continuously, not just at the end.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A one-time final step only — It is not a one-time final step.
• Something done only by physicians — Nurses carry out evaluation, not only physicians.
• Unrelated to the original care plan — It is directly related to the original care plan's goals.

⭐ Key point: Evaluation outcomes: goal met, partially met, or not met.$x$, null),
(156, $x$Which activity best describes the evaluation phase?$x$, $x$Reassess and compare outcomes with the set objectives$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Reassess and compare outcomes with the set objectives
The EVALUATION phase is the final step of the nursing process (ADPIE), but it is ongoing. The nurse REASSESSES the patient (collects new data) and COMPARES the actual OUTCOMES with the expected outcomes (objectives/goals) set during planning. Based on this, the nurse decides whether the goal is MET, PARTIALLY MET or NOT MET, and whether to continue, modify or end the care plan.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Interpret data and identify client problems — Interpreting data and identifying client problems is the DIAGNOSIS phase.
• Establish short- and long-term goals — Establishing short- and long-term goals is the PLANNING phase.
• Carry out nursing procedures and document — Carrying out nursing procedures and documenting them is the IMPLEMENTATION phase.

⭐ Key point: If a goal is not met, ask why: was the diagnosis wrong, the goal unrealistic, or the interventions ineffective?$x$, null),
(157, $x$Copy-pasting a previous nursing note into today's entry without verifying current accuracy risks:$x$, $x$Documentation errors and propagation of outdated or inaccurate information ('note bloat')$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Documentation errors and propagation of outdated or inaccurate information ('note bloat')
COPY-PASTING previous notes without checking accuracy risks DOCUMENTATION ERRORS: outdated or wrong information is repeated ("note bloat"), real changes may be missed, and the record may misrepresent the patient's current condition. This is unsafe and can have legal consequences.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Improved efficiency with no downside — It may seem efficient, but has serious downsides.
• Better legal protection — It weakens, not improves, legal protection.
• Faster patient discharge — It doesn't speed up discharge.

⭐ Key point: Document only what you have personally assessed or done, at the time.$x$, null),
(158, $x$How can nurses contribute to quality assurance efforts?$x$, $x$By participating in quality improvement initiatives and adhering to best practices$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: By participating in quality improvement initiatives and adhering to best practices
QUALITY ASSURANCE (QA) and quality improvement aim to make care safe, effective and patient-centred. Nurses contribute by: PARTICIPATING in QUALITY IMPROVEMENT INITIATIVES (audits, data collection, quality committees, PDSA cycles, care bundles), ADHERING to BEST PRACTICES and evidence-based guidelines, reporting incidents and near misses, listening to patient feedback, keeping accurate documentation, and continuing professional development.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• By ignoring feedback from patients — Ignoring patient feedback misses opportunities to improve care.
• By working independently of their colleagues — Quality improvement requires teamwork and collaboration, not working alone.
• By prioritizing speed over accuracy — Prioritising speed over accuracy causes errors and harms patients.

⭐ Key point: Every nurse is responsible for quality — not just managers.$x$, null),
(159, $x$A nurse repositions a patient who complains of difficulty breathing. This is which phase of the nursing process?$x$, $x$Implementation$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Implementation
The IMPLEMENTATION phase of the nursing process is when the nurse CARRIES OUT the planned nursing interventions. REPOSITIONING a patient who has DIFFICULTY BREATHING (e.g. sitting them upright or in high Fowler's position to improve lung expansion) is a nursing ACTION — so it is IMPLEMENTATION. After this, the nurse would EVALUATE whether breathing has improved (respiratory rate, SpO2, work of breathing).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Assessment — Assessment is collecting data (e.g. listening to the patient's complaint, measuring SpO2).
• Evaluation — Evaluation is checking the response after the action.
• Outcome identification — Outcome identification is part of planning (setting goals).

⭐ Key point: Implementation includes independent, dependent and collaborative nursing actions, followed by documentation.$x$, null),
(160, $x$A Nurse at HMC asked a patient to verbalise if he understood the information given during nursing care to the client according to a devised nursing care plan. During which step of the nursing process does such action take place?$x$, $x$Evaluation$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Evaluation
Asking the patient to VERBALISE (explain in their own words) whether they UNDERSTOOD the information given during care checks whether the planned TEACHING OUTCOME has been achieved. This is EVALUATION — comparing the patient's response with the expected outcome (e.g. "The patient will verbalise understanding of their medication regimen").

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Assessment — Assessment is collecting initial data about the patient's needs.
• Planning — Planning is setting goals and choosing interventions.
• Implementation — Implementation is giving the teaching itself.

⭐ Key point: The "teach-back" method is an effective way to evaluate understanding.$x$, null),
(161, $x$Effective discharge planning for a rehabilitation patient should begin:$x$, $x$Early during the hospital stay, involving the patient, family, and multidisciplinary team$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Early during the hospital stay, involving the patient, family, and multidisciplinary team
Effective DISCHARGE PLANNING should BEGIN EARLY during the hospital stay (ideally on admission), and involve the PATIENT, FAMILY and MULTIDISCIPLINARY TEAM (physiotherapy, occupational therapy, social work, dietitian), to prepare for a safe transition home or to another facility.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Only on the day of discharge — The day of discharge is too late.
• After the patient has already left the facility — After discharge is too late.
• Without involving the patient's family — Family involvement is important.

⭐ Key point: Early discharge planning reduces delays and readmissions.$x$, null),
(162, $x$The goal of quality assurance in nursing is to:$x$, $x$Continuously improve patient care and outcomes$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Continuously improve patient care and outcomes
The main goal of QUALITY ASSURANCE (QA) in nursing is to CONTINUOUSLY IMPROVE PATIENT CARE and OUTCOMES. It involves setting standards, measuring performance (structure, process and outcome), comparing results with standards, identifying gaps, and making improvements, then re-measuring. QA aims for excellence, not just the minimum.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Reduce the cost of care — Reducing costs may be a benefit, but it is not the main goal.
• Meet the minimum legal standards of care — Meeting only minimum legal standards is not enough; QA aims higher.
• Ensure nurses follow all orders without question — Following orders without question is unsafe; nurses must use professional judgement.

⭐ Key point: Quality tools include audits, incident reporting, PDSA cycles, care bundles and patient satisfaction surveys.$x$, null),
(163, $x$The final step of the research process is typically considered to be$x$, $x$Disseminating or reporting the research findings to relevant audiences$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Disseminating or reporting the research findings to relevant audiences
The RESEARCH PROCESS typically follows these steps: identify the problem, review the literature, develop a framework, formulate objectives/hypotheses, choose a design, select the sample, collect data, analyse data, interpret findings, and finally DISSEMINATE (communicate) the FINDINGS through reports, journal articles, conference presentations or workshops, so that others can use them to improve practice.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Formulating the research hypothesis — Formulating hypotheses is an early step.
• Reviewing related literature — Reviewing literature is an early step (though it continues throughout).
• Selecting the sample — Selecting the sample is a middle step.

⭐ Key point: Research is only useful if its findings are shared and applied in practice (research utilisation).$x$, null),
(164, $x$Number of ICU admissions per month at a hospital is an example of which scale of measurement?$x$, $x$Ratio$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Ratio
Data are measured on four scales: NOMINAL (categories without order), ORDINAL (ranked categories), INTERVAL (equal intervals, no true zero), and RATIO (equal intervals AND a TRUE ZERO). The NUMBER OF ICU ADMISSIONS per month is a count: zero admissions means none at all (true zero), and 20 admissions is twice as many as 10. So it is RATIO data.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Nominal — Nominal data are categories (e.g. blood group, sex).
• Ordinal — Ordinal data are ranks (e.g. pain mild/moderate/severe).
• Interval — Interval data have no true zero (e.g. temperature in °C).

⭐ Key point: Ratio data allow all statistical calculations, including ratios ("twice as many").$x$, null),
(165, $x$A study finds a statistically significant but very small difference between two treatments (e.g. a 1% difference in outcome) in a very large sample. This illustrates the important distinction between:$x$, $x$Statistical significance and clinical significance/relevance$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Statistical significance and clinical significance/relevance
STATISTICAL SIGNIFICANCE (p < 0.05) means the result is unlikely due to chance. In a VERY LARGE sample, even TINY differences can be statistically significant. CLINICAL SIGNIFICANCE asks whether the difference is LARGE ENOUGH to MATTER to patients. A 1% difference may be statistically significant but not clinically important.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Reliability and validity, which are unrelated concepts here — Reliability and validity are about measurement.
• Qualitative and quantitative research — This isn't about qualitative vs quantitative.
• Ethics and methodology — This isn't about ethics vs methodology.

⭐ Key point: Always ask: "Is this difference meaningful for patients?"$x$, null),
(166, $x$The Latin term 'Ibid' is used when citing:$x$, $x$The same work, a different page from the last citation$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: The same work, a different page from the last citation
"IBID." (from Latin ibidem, "in the same place") is used in footnote referencing to refer to the SAME WORK cited in the IMMEDIATELY PRECEDING note. In the teaching used for this exam, "Ibid" is used for the same work with a DIFFERENT PAGE (with the new page number given), while "loc. cit." is used for the same work and same page, and "op. cit." for a work cited earlier but not immediately before. (Many modern styles, such as APA, do not use these terms.)

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The same work, the same page as just cited — In this teaching, "same work, same page" is "loc. cit." rather than ibid.
• A completely different, unrelated work — A completely different work would be cited in full.
• Only works published this year — Ibid is not limited by publication year.

⭐ Key point: Follow the referencing style required by your institution.$x$, null),
(167, $x$In defining the scope of a study, which is NOT necessarily indicated?$x$, $x$The significance of the study$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: The significance of the study
The SCOPE of a study defines its boundaries: the PERIOD (time frame), the POPULATION (who), and the SETTING (where), and sometimes the variables studied. The SIGNIFICANCE of the study (why it is important) is a separate section, not part of the scope.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The period of study — The period IS part of the scope.
• The population of study — The population IS part of the scope.
• The setting of the study — The setting IS part of the scope.

⭐ Key point: Scope and limitations are usually stated in Chapter One of a research project.$x$, null),
(168, $x$Process of research means the following EXCEPT$x$, $x$Making policies$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Making policies
The RESEARCH PROCESS involves systematic steps to generate knowledge: IDENTIFYING a PROBLEM, DEFINING it precisely and unambiguously, reviewing literature, formulating objectives and hypotheses, DESIGNING the research, selecting a sample, collecting and analysing data, interpreting results, and disseminating findings. MAKING POLICIES is NOT part of the research process — policies are made by managers or governments, who may USE research findings as evidence.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Identifying a problem — Identifying a problem IS part of the research process.
• Defining the problem in precise terms in unambiguous terms — Defining the problem precisely IS part of the process.
• Designing the research — Designing the research IS part of the process.

⭐ Key point: Evidence-based policy uses research findings, but policy-making itself is a separate process.$x$, null),
(169, $x$A core ethical requirement in human subjects research is that participation must be:$x$, $x$Voluntary, based on informed consent, without coercion$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Voluntary, based on informed consent, without coercion
A core ethical requirement of research with human participants is that participation must be VOLUNTARY, based on INFORMED CONSENT, and WITHOUT COERCION. Participants must be told the purpose, procedures, risks, benefits, confidentiality arrangements, and their RIGHT to REFUSE or WITHDRAW at any time without penalty (e.g. their care will not be affected). Consent should be documented (usually signed).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Mandatory for all eligible patients — Participation can never be mandatory.
• Compensated financially regardless of consent — Payment does not replace consent; excessive payment can be coercive.
• Determined solely by the researcher — The researcher cannot decide for participants; they decide for themselves.

⭐ Key point: Extra protection is needed for vulnerable groups (children, unconscious ICU patients, prisoners), e.g. consent from legal representatives.$x$, null),
(170, $x$The Belmont Report's 3 core ethical principles are:$x$, $x$Autonomy, Beneficence, Justice$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Autonomy, Beneficence, Justice
The BELMONT REPORT (USA, 1979) set out three core ethical principles for research with human subjects: (1) RESPECT FOR PERSONS (AUTONOMY) — treat people as autonomous, obtain informed consent, and protect those with reduced autonomy; (2) BENEFICENCE — maximise benefits and minimise harm; (3) JUSTICE — fair selection of participants and fair distribution of research burdens and benefits.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Consent, Privacy, Fairness — Consent, privacy and fairness are applications of the principles, not the three principles themselves.
• Honesty, Accuracy, Transparency — Honesty, accuracy and transparency relate to research integrity.
• Safety, Dignity, Respect — Safety, dignity and respect are general values.

⭐ Key point: Research ethics committees use these principles when reviewing study proposals.$x$, null),
(171, $x$Which measure of central tendency is appropriate for nominal (categorical) data, such as the most common blood group among patients in a ward?$x$, $x$Mode$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Mode
NOMINAL data are CATEGORIES with no order (e.g. blood groups A, B, AB, O; sex; ward type). You cannot calculate an average of "blood group A" and "blood group O", and there is no "middle" category. The only meaningful measure of central tendency is the MODE — the MOST FREQUENTLY occurring category (e.g. "blood group O is the most common").

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Mean — The MEAN requires numerical (interval/ratio) data.
• Median — The MEDIAN requires data that can be ordered (ordinal or higher).
• Standard deviation — STANDARD DEVIATION is a measure of spread, not central tendency, and requires numerical data.

⭐ Key point: Nominal data are described using frequencies, percentages, and bar or pie charts.$x$, null),
(172, $x$Common electronic databases for nurse researchers include all EXCEPT:$x$, $x$American Nurses Association$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: American Nurses Association
Nurse researchers use ELECTRONIC DATABASES to search for published literature: CINAHL (nursing and allied health), PUBMED (free search interface for biomedical literature), MEDLINE (the main biomedical database, accessed via PubMed), Cochrane Library, Embase and PsycINFO. The AMERICAN NURSES ASSOCIATION (ANA) is a PROFESSIONAL ORGANISATION, not a literature database (although it publishes standards and journals).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• CINAHL — CINAHL IS a database.
• PubMed — PubMed IS a database search engine.
• MEDLINE — MEDLINE IS a database.

⭐ Key point: Use clear search terms and Boolean operators (AND, OR, NOT) to find relevant studies efficiently.$x$, null),
(173, $x$A nurse researcher wants to study the lived experience of ICU patients after discharge. The most appropriate research approach for this question is:$x$, $x$A qualitative approach (e.g. interviews) to capture rich, descriptive experiential data$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: A qualitative approach (e.g. interviews) to capture rich, descriptive experiential data
To study the LIVED EXPERIENCE of ICU patients after discharge, a QUALITATIVE approach (e.g. in-depth INTERVIEWS, phenomenology) is most appropriate, because it captures rich, descriptive data about people's experiences and meanings.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A strictly quantitative, numerical survey with no narrative data — A purely numerical survey can't capture lived experience in depth.
• No research method is appropriate for this topic — Research is possible on this topic.
• A laboratory experiment — A laboratory experiment is not appropriate.

⭐ Key point: Qualitative findings help nurses understand patients' perspectives and improve care.$x$, null),
(174, $x$A hospital's 'departmental risk management programme' typically includes all of the following EXCEPT:$x$, $x$Concealing incidents to protect the department's reputation$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Concealing incidents to protect the department's reputation
A departmental RISK MANAGEMENT PROGRAMME aims to identify, prevent and reduce harm. It includes: IDENTIFICATION of common RISK categories (falls, medication errors, infections, pressure ulcers, equipment failure), INCIDENT REPORTING mechanisms, analysis of incidents (e.g. root cause analysis), corrective actions, staff education, and MONITORING and EVALUATION of interventions. CONCEALING INCIDENTS to protect the department's reputation is unethical and dangerous — it prevents learning, repeats harm, and may be illegal.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Identification of common risk categories — Identifying risk categories IS part of the programme.
• Incident reporting mechanisms — Incident reporting IS part of the programme.
• Monitoring and evaluation of interventions — Monitoring and evaluation IS part of the programme.

⭐ Key point: Openness (duty of candour) with patients and families after incidents builds trust.$x$, null),
(175, $x$The conflict resolution style where one party gives in to preserve the relationship is called:$x$, $x$Accommodating$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Accommodating
ACCOMMODATING (smoothing/obliging) is a conflict-resolution style where one party GIVES IN to the other's wishes to PRESERVE THE RELATIONSHIP and keep harmony ("I lose, you win"). It is useful when the issue matters more to the other person, when keeping good relationships is more important, or when you realise you are wrong. Overuse can lead to resentment and unmet needs.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Competing — COMPETING is pursuing your own goals at the other's expense ("win-lose").
• Compromising — COMPROMISING is both parties giving up something ("split the difference").
• Avoiding — AVOIDING is ignoring or withdrawing from the conflict.

⭐ Key point: Thomas-Kilmann's five styles: competing, collaborating, compromising, avoiding and accommodating.$x$, null),
(176, $x$Which of the following is a key responsibility of a nurse manager during disaster preparedness?$x$, $x$Develop, communicate, and regularly evaluate disaster response plans$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Develop, communicate, and regularly evaluate disaster response plans
DISASTER PREPAREDNESS is a key nurse manager responsibility. The manager should help DEVELOP disaster response PLANS (roles, surge capacity, triage areas, staff call-in lists, supplies and equipment, communication systems, evacuation routes), COMMUNICATE them to all staff (training, briefings), and REGULARLY EVALUATE them through drills and exercises, updating the plan based on lessons learned.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Close the hospital permanently — Closing the hospital permanently is not disaster preparedness.
• Cancel emergency drills — Cancelling drills prevents staff from being ready.
• Delegate all planning to external agencies — Planning should be done internally with input from external agencies, not fully delegated.

⭐ Key point: Regular drills reveal weaknesses before a real disaster occurs.$x$, null),
(177, $x$A nurse floats to an unfamiliar ICU due to staffing shortages and is assigned a complex ventilated patient. The most appropriate first action is to:$x$, $x$Communicate honestly about their competency level with this patient type, request orientation/support as needed, and advocate for patient safety$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Communicate honestly about their competency level with this patient type, request orientation/support as needed, and advocate for patient safety
When floated to an unfamiliar ICU, the nurse should COMMUNICATE HONESTLY about their COMPETENCY, REQUEST ORIENTATION or SUPPORT, and ADVOCATE for PATIENT SAFETY.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Refuse the assignment entirely with no discussion — Refusing without discussion is unprofessional.
• Accept the assignment silently despite feeling unprepared, saying nothing — Accepting silently despite being unprepared risks patient safety.
• Provide care without seeking any support or clarification — Providing care without support may be unsafe.

⭐ Key point: Nurses must practise within their competence.$x$, null),
(178, $x$Which scholar introduced the functional type of organisation?$x$, $x$F.W. Taylor$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: F.W. Taylor
Frederick Winslow TAYLOR, the father of scientific management, introduced the FUNCTIONAL TYPE OF ORGANISATION ("functional foremanship"). Instead of one supervisor controlling everything, each worker received instructions from several SPECIALIST supervisors, each responsible for a particular function (e.g. planning, speed, quality, maintenance). This improved specialisation and efficiency, but could confuse workers with multiple bosses (breaking unity of command).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Chester Bernard — Chester Barnard wrote about the functions of the executive and acceptance of authority.
• Allen — "Allen" is not associated with the functional organisation.
• Max Weber — Max Weber developed the theory of BUREAUCRACY.

⭐ Key point: In hospitals, nurses often report to both a ward manager and specialist leads — a form of functional organisation.$x$, null),
(179, $x$The major concern of an effective leader is:$x$, $x$Concern for both production and employees$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Concern for both production and employees
An effective leader shows CONCERN for BOTH PRODUCTION (achieving goals — e.g. safe, high-quality patient care, efficient use of resources) AND EMPLOYEES (staff wellbeing, development, motivation and relationships). In Blake and Mouton's MANAGERIAL GRID, this is the "TEAM MANAGEMENT" (9,9) style — considered the most effective, because committed, supported staff achieve better results.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Concern for production only — Concern for production only ("task management") can burn out staff.
• Concern for self — Concern for self is selfish and ineffective.
• Concern for employees only — Concern for employees only ("country club management") may neglect goals.

⭐ Key point: Effective nurse leaders balance task demands with staff support.$x$, null),
(180, $x$During a mass casualty event, the charge nurse must delegate tasks rapidly to a team with mixed experience levels. The most appropriate delegation approach is to:$x$, $x$Match tasks to each team member's competency level, ensuring appropriate supervision for less experienced staff$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Match tasks to each team member's competency level, ensuring appropriate supervision for less experienced staff
During a MASS CASUALTY event, the charge nurse should MATCH TASKS to each team member's COMPETENCY level, with APPROPRIATE SUPERVISION for less experienced staff.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Assign tasks randomly with no regard for skill level — Random assignment is unsafe.
• Assign only the most complex tasks to the least experienced staff — Complex tasks for inexperienced staff is unsafe.
• Refuse to delegate anything and do all tasks personally — Refusing to delegate is impossible in mass casualties.

⭐ Key point: Use the five rights of delegation.$x$, null),
(181, $x$The following are good qualities of a leader EXCEPT:$x$, $x$Rich$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Rich
Good leaders are VISIONARY (have a clear picture of the future and inspire others towards it), KNOWLEDGEABLE (clinically and managerially competent), and have INTEGRITY (honesty, fairness, consistency, ethical behaviour). Other qualities: good communication, empathy, decisiveness, accountability and humility. Being RICH (wealthy) is NOT a leadership quality — leadership depends on character, skills and behaviour, not money.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Visionary — Visionary IS a leadership quality.
• Knowledgeable — Knowledgeable IS a leadership quality.
• Integrity — Integrity IS a leadership quality.

⭐ Key point: Leadership can be developed through training, mentoring and experience.$x$, null),
(182, $x$Who advocated the fourteen principles of management?$x$, $x$Henri Fayol$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Henri Fayol
Henri FAYOL (1841–1925), a French mining engineer, developed the 14 PRINCIPLES OF MANAGEMENT in his book "General and Industrial Management" (1916): division of work, authority and responsibility, discipline, unity of command, unity of direction, subordination of individual interests to the general interest, remuneration, centralisation, scalar chain, order, equity, stability of tenure, initiative, and esprit de corps.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Frederick Taylor — Frederick Taylor developed SCIENTIFIC MANAGEMENT.
• Max Weber — Max Weber developed the theory of BUREAUCRACY.
• Karl Marx — Karl Marx was a philosopher and economist, not a management theorist.

⭐ Key point: Fayol also described the five functions of management: planning, organising, commanding, coordinating and controlling.$x$, null),
(183, $x$The nurse manager uses the results of a nursing audit to make changes to the unit's policies. This is an example of the nurse's role in:$x$, $x$Quality improvement$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Quality improvement
A NURSING AUDIT systematically reviews nursing care (often through records) against agreed standards. When the nurse manager USES AUDIT RESULTS to CHANGE unit POLICIES and practices, this is QUALITY IMPROVEMENT — using data to identify gaps and make changes that improve care. The audit cycle: set standards → measure practice → compare with standards → implement changes → re-audit.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Punishing staff for poor performance — Audits are not for punishing staff; they identify system issues.
• Maintaining the status quo — Changing policies based on audit is the opposite of maintaining the status quo.
• Disregarding the documentation process — Audits depend on, rather than disregard, documentation.

⭐ Key point: Involve staff in audit and change — they are more likely to support improvements they helped design.$x$, null),
(184, $x$Which of the following is a function of a ICU manager?$x$, $x$Motivation$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Motivation
MOTIVATION is a key FUNCTION of an ICU manager (part of directing/leading): inspiring and encouraging staff to work well and develop professionally, through recognition, fair treatment, good working conditions, opportunities for training and promotion, involvement in decisions, and support during stressful situations. Motivated staff provide safer, better-quality patient care and are less likely to leave.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Bureaucracy — Bureaucracy is an organisational structure with rigid rules, not a manager's function.
• Humility — Humility is a personal quality, not a function.
• Oppression — Oppression is never an appropriate management behaviour.

⭐ Key point: Herzberg: recognition, responsibility and achievement motivate; pay and conditions prevent dissatisfaction.$x$, null),
(185, $x$Below are the Patient and family-focused interventions EXCEPT$x$, $x$Documentation of consent$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Documentation of consent
PATIENT- and FAMILY-FOCUSED interventions address emotional and psychological needs: RELIEVING ANXIETY and providing a SENSE OF SECURITY, ALLOWING FAMILY to stay with the patient where possible, providing clear EXPLANATIONS and INFORMATION, involving family in care and decisions, and respecting cultural and spiritual needs. DOCUMENTATION OF CONSENT is an important LEGAL/administrative task, but it is not a patient- or family-focused supportive intervention.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Relieve anxiety and provide a sense of security — Relieving anxiety IS a patient-focused intervention.
• Allow family to stay with patient, if possible, to alleviate anxiety — Allowing family presence IS family-focused.
• Provide explanations and information — Providing explanations IS patient- and family-focused.

⭐ Key point: Family-centred care in ICU reduces anxiety and improves satisfaction and outcomes.$x$, null),
(186, $x$When triaging multiple patients, a nurse practising justice should prioritise based on:$x$, $x$Clinical need$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Clinical need
The ethical principle of JUSTICE requires fair treatment of all patients. In TRIAGE, a nurse practising justice prioritises patients based on CLINICAL NEED — the most seriously ill or injured, who will benefit most from immediate care, are seen first — not on personal characteristics, wealth, social status, or insurance.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Personal characteristics such as wealth — Wealth and personal characteristics should never determine care priority.
• Order of arrival only, regardless of severity — Order of arrival alone ignores severity and could let critically ill patients deteriorate.
• Insurance status — Insurance status should not decide clinical priority.

⭐ Key point: Use validated triage tools (e.g. South African Triage Scale, ESI) for fair, consistent prioritisation.$x$, null),
(187, $x$The key distinction between Justice and Fairness is that:$x$, $x$Justice = rightness/what's deserved; Fairness = impartiality/equal treatment$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Justice = rightness/what's deserved; Fairness = impartiality/equal treatment
JUSTICE and FAIRNESS are related but slightly different: JUSTICE refers to RIGHTNESS — giving people what they DESERVE or are entitled to, and distributing benefits and burdens appropriately. FAIRNESS refers to IMPARTIALITY — treating people EQUALLY and without bias or favouritism. In nursing, both guide ethical decisions about care and resources.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• They mean exactly the same thing with no difference — They are closely related but not exactly the same.
• Justice only applies to law, never to nursing — Justice applies to nursing ethics (e.g. resource allocation), not only to law.
• Fairness only applies to financial matters — Fairness applies to all aspects of care, not just finances.

⭐ Key point: Equity (giving people what they need) is often what justice requires, beyond simple equal treatment.$x$, null),
(188, $x$Applying the exact same rule to everyone regardless of their different starting points is:$x$, $x$Justice, but not necessarily truly fair to everyone$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Justice, but not necessarily truly fair to everyone
Applying the SAME RULE to everyone regardless of DIFFERENT STARTING POINTS may be just (consistent), but NOT NECESSARILY FAIR (equitable) — some people need more support to achieve the same outcome.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Always guaranteed to be fair — Equal treatment is not always fair.
• Never a valid ethical approach — It can be a valid approach in some cases.
• Only relevant in legal settings — It applies beyond legal settings.

⭐ Key point: Equity means giving people what they need to achieve fair outcomes.$x$, null),
(189, $x$The core legal/professional principle behind nursing documentation is best summarised as:$x$, $x$'If it wasn't documented, it wasn't done'$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 'If it wasn't documented, it wasn't done'
The legal and professional principle "IF IT WASN'T DOCUMENTED, IT WASN'T DONE" means that in legal proceedings, audits or investigations, care that is not recorded is assumed not to have been given. Documentation is evidence of care, communicates information to the team, supports continuity of care, and protects both patient and nurse.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Documentation is optional if care was given correctly — Documentation is never optional, even if care was correct.
• Only negative events need to be documented — All care, observations and events should be documented, not only negative events.
• Documentation can be completed weeks later without issue — Documentation must be timely (contemporaneous); late entries must be clearly marked.

⭐ Key point: Write accurate, clear, factual, timely and signed records.$x$, null),
(190, $x$The ethical principle of justice, as applied to resource allocation in critical care, refers to:$x$, $x$Fair and equitable distribution of limited healthcare resources$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Fair and equitable distribution of limited healthcare resources
JUSTICE in critical care means FAIR and EQUITABLE DISTRIBUTION of LIMITED resources (ICU beds, ventilators, dialysis machines, staff time, expensive drugs) based on clinical need and likely benefit, using transparent and consistent criteria.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Treating all patients identically regardless of need — Treating everyone identically ignores different needs.
• Prioritising the wealthiest patients — Prioritising wealthy patients is unjust.
• Ignoring resource limitations entirely — Ignoring resource limits is unrealistic and can harm patients.

⭐ Key point: Triage and admission policies, made openly by senior clinicians, support just allocation.$x$, null),
(191, $x$Which ethical principle refers to a patient's right to make their own healthcare decisions?$x$, $x$Autonomy$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Autonomy
AUTONOMY is the ethical principle that recognises a competent patient's RIGHT to MAKE THEIR OWN HEALTHCARE DECISIONS — including accepting or refusing treatment — based on adequate information and free from coercion. Nurses support autonomy through informed consent, honest communication, respecting choices and advance directives, and advocating for patients' wishes.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Beneficence — Beneficence means acting for the patient's good.
• Non-maleficence — Non-maleficence means avoiding harm.
• Justice — Justice means fairness.

⭐ Key point: When patients lack capacity, decisions are made in their best interests, considering their known wishes.$x$, null),
(192, $x$Which is one of the twin Latin maxims applied in the principles of fair hearing?$x$, $x$Nemo judex in causa sua$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Nemo judex in causa sua
The two Latin maxims of FAIR HEARING (natural justice) are: "NEMO JUDEX IN CAUSA SUA" (no one should be a judge in their own case — no bias) and "AUDI ALTERAM PARTEM" (hear the other side).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Nemo dat quod non habet — "Nemo dat quod non habet" relates to property law.
• Ubi jus ibi remedium — "Ubi jus ibi remedium" means where there is a right, there is a remedy.
• Res ipsa loquitur — "Res ipsa loquitur" means the thing speaks for itself.

⭐ Key point: Fair hearing applies in disciplinary proceedings.$x$, null),
(193, $x$An advance directive is a document that:$x$, $x$Expresses a patient's wishes for future medical care if they become unable to decide for themselves$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Expresses a patient's wishes for future medical care if they become unable to decide for themselves
An ADVANCE DIRECTIVE expresses a patient's wishes for FUTURE MEDICAL CARE if they become UNABLE TO DECIDE (e.g. refusing resuscitation or ventilation).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Is only valid after the patient's death — It is valid while the patient is alive.
• Can only be created by a physician — Patients create it, not physicians.
• Overrides all patient autonomy while the patient is conscious — It supports autonomy.

⭐ Key point: Check for advance directives on admission.$x$, null),
(194, $x$A dying patient has Cheyne-Stokes respirations. The nurse educates the family that this is:$x$, $x$Normal at end-of-life$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Normal at end-of-life
CHEYNE-STOKES respiration is a cyclical breathing pattern: breaths gradually get deeper and faster, then shallower and slower, followed by a period of APNOEA (no breathing), then the cycle repeats. It is caused by changes in the brain's control of breathing and is common and NORMAL near the END OF LIFE. Educating the family that this is an expected part of dying (and not a sign of suffering) helps reduce their distress.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A sign of pain — It is not a sign of pain.
• Indicative of fluid overload — It does not indicate fluid overload.
• Reversible with oxygen — It is not reversible with oxygen at end of life.

⭐ Key point: Focus on comfort: positioning, mouth care, calm environment, and supporting the family.$x$, null),
(195, $x$Which nursing action best supports early identification of a TPN-related infection?$x$, $x$Assessing the catheter insertion site for redness, warmth, swelling, or drainage at each shift, along with monitoring temperature trends$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Assessing the catheter insertion site for redness, warmth, swelling, or drainage at each shift, along with monitoring temperature trends
Early identification of TPN-related (central line) infection involves ASSESSING the INSERTION SITE for REDNESS, WARMTH, SWELLING or DRAINAGE each shift, and MONITORING TEMPERATURE trends.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Assessing the insertion site only once weekly during dressing changes — Weekly checks are too infrequent.
• Relying solely on the patient's subjective report of pain — Subjective reports alone are not reliable.
• Checking the site only if the patient develops hypotension — Waiting for hypotension is too late.

⭐ Key point: Use strict aseptic technique and a dedicated lumen for TPN.$x$, null),
(196, $x$These are risks of parenteral nutrition EXCEPT$x$, $x$Vomiting$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Vomiting
PARENTERAL NUTRITION (PN/TPN) is given into a vein (usually a central line). Its risks include: INFECTION (central line bloodstream infection — the solution is rich in glucose and lipids, which support bacterial growth), HYPERGLYCAEMIA (high glucose content), HYPOGLYCAEMIA (if PN is stopped suddenly — rebound), electrolyte imbalances, refeeding syndrome, fluid overload, liver dysfunction, and line complications (thrombosis, pneumothorax). VOMITING is not a typical risk because PN bypasses the gut.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Infection — Infection IS a major risk.
• Hyperglycaemia — Hyperglycaemia IS a common risk.
• Hypoglycaemia — Hypoglycaemia IS a risk if PN is stopped abruptly.

⭐ Key point: Use a dedicated lumen, strict asepsis, and monitor glucose and electrolytes daily.$x$, null),
(197, $x$The 'ebb and flow' model of the metabolic response to trauma describes which sequence?$x$, $x$An initial hypometabolic 'ebb' phase of hypoperfusion followed by a hypermetabolic 'flow' (catabolic) phase$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: An initial hypometabolic 'ebb' phase of hypoperfusion followed by a hypermetabolic 'flow' (catabolic) phase
The "EBB and FLOW" model describes the metabolic response to trauma: an initial EBB phase (first 24–48 hours) with HYPOMETABOLISM and hypoperfusion (shock), followed by a FLOW phase with HYPERMETABOLISM and catabolism (muscle breakdown) during recovery.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A single sustained hypermetabolic phase with no early depression — There is an early depressed phase.
• An initial anabolic phase followed by a hypometabolic phase — The anabolic phase comes later in recovery.
• A random, unpredictable pattern with no identifiable phases — The pattern is predictable.

⭐ Key point: Nutritional support is important during the flow phase.$x$, null),
(198, $x$Refeeding syndrome is a risk primarily in patients who:$x$, $x$Have experienced prolonged starvation or severe malnutrition before reintroducing nutrition$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Have experienced prolonged starvation or severe malnutrition before reintroducing nutrition
REFEEDING SYNDROME is a potentially fatal shift of fluids and electrolytes that occurs when nutrition is restarted in patients after PROLONGED STARVATION or SEVERE MALNUTRITION (e.g. anorexia, chronic alcoholism, prolonged fasting, cancer cachexia). Insulin rises, driving PHOSPHATE, POTASSIUM and MAGNESIUM into cells, causing dangerously low blood levels, which can cause heart failure, arrhythmias, respiratory failure, confusion and death.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Have been well-nourished throughout admission — Well-nourished patients are not at significant risk.
• Are only mildly overweight — Mildly overweight patients are not at particular risk.
• Have normal phosphate levels only — Normal phosphate levels are not the issue; low levels are.

⭐ Key point: Prevent by starting feeds slowly (e.g. 10 kcal/kg/day), giving thiamine, and monitoring electrolytes daily.$x$, null),
(199, $x$Contraindications to chest physiotherapy include all of the following EXCEPT:$x$, $x$Mild, stable chronic bronchitis$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Mild, stable chronic bronchitis
CHEST PHYSIOTHERAPY (percussion, vibration, postural drainage) can be harmful in certain conditions. Contraindications include: UNTREATED PNEUMOTHORAX (could worsen it), ACTIVE HAEMOPTYSIS (coughing blood — could increase bleeding), UNCONTROLLED RAISED ICP (head-down positions and coughing raise ICP), unstable spinal injury, rib fractures and haemodynamic instability. MILD, STABLE CHRONIC BRONCHITIS is NOT a contraindication — physiotherapy can help clear secretions.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Untreated pneumothorax — Untreated pneumothorax IS a contraindication.
• Active haemoptysis — Active haemoptysis IS a contraindication.
• Uncontrolled raised intracranial pressure — Uncontrolled raised ICP IS a contraindication.

⭐ Key point: Always assess the patient's stability before chest physiotherapy.$x$, null),
(200, $x$On the MRC muscle strength scale, a score of 0 indicates:$x$, $x$Complete paralysis, no movement$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Complete paralysis, no movement
The MRC (Medical Research Council) muscle strength scale grades power from 0 to 5: 0 = NO CONTRACTION (COMPLETE PARALYSIS); 1 = flicker or trace of contraction; 2 = movement with gravity eliminated; 3 = movement against gravity; 4 = movement against gravity and some resistance; 5 = normal power. So 0 means complete paralysis with no movement.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Normal power — Normal power is grade 5.
• Movement against gravity only — Movement against gravity only is grade 3.
• Flicker of movement only — A flicker of movement is grade 1.

⭐ Key point: The MRC sum score (0–60, testing 6 muscle groups on both sides) is used to diagnose ICU-acquired weakness (below 48).$x$, null)
),
upd as (
  update public.questions qq
     set exp = r.exp,
         ans_idx = coalesce((select (t.i - 1)::int from jsonb_array_elements_text(qq.opts) with ordinality t(v, i) where t.v = r.fixed_answer), qq.ans_idx)
    from r
   where qq.q = r.q
     and qq.opts ->> qq.ans_idx = r.answer
  returning r.n
)
select count(distinct n) as "questions updated (should be 100)", count(*) as "rows changed" from upd;
